  "subtitle": "The first ATM that closed its own face on you: a self-closing safety-case window, a magnetic card you insert stripe-up, and a machine that could seize your card and keep it",
  "why_scout_cares": "The museum curates strangers talking to machines. The IBM 3614 is the purest early embodiment of that: a full-banking ATM whose interface hides behind a powered protective shutter that opens to accept your card and closes itself 25 seconds after you finish — the machine literally shuts its own face on you. Conversations with the machine happened around a card you slide in stripe-up, the reverse of every modern ATM, and it could physically retain a suspected-stolen card and trigger an alarm. This is the self-service-banking / ATM gap in the collection, a completely untapped interactive family, and it has a verified public-domain period photograph.",
